.\" Generated by "sysml -man"; edit the description in the command's usage.go.
.TH SYSML 1 "2026-08-30" "OpenSysML" "OpenSysML Manual"
.SH NAME
sysml \- run, check, convert and render SysML v2 and KerML models
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysml
[options] [file...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
sysml loads the models it is given \(em a file, a directory to walk or a glob
\(em as a single model, so a declaration in one file resolves against the
others whichever order they were named in. With no expression or check to
carry out it opens an interactive prompt; otherwise it does what was asked and
exits on the verdict, which is what lets a run gate a build.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-action " value"
Run this action to completion, as \-action "Drive rover1" to run it on an
object (repeatable)
.TP
.BI \-advance " value"
Simulated time units to run each \-state machine for (default: only its
initial transition)
.TP
.BI \-calc " value"
Invoke this calculation and report what it computed, as \-calc "Fall(3, 4)"
(repeatable)
.TP
.BI \-constraint " value"
Evaluate this constraint and exit (repeatable)
.TP
.BI \-convert " string"
Convert the model to this format instead of running it: sysml, kerml, ttl,
turtle or rdf (RDF is experimental)
.TP
.BI \-cpuprofile " string"
Write a CPU profile of the run to this file, for go tool pprof
.TP
.B \-debug
Report every diagnostic over the whole session buffer, with the pass that
produced it
.TP
.BI \-doc\-form " string"
Form \-render\-document writes: markdown (default) or pdf, which drives an
external converter
.TP
.BI \-e " value"
Evaluate expression and exit (shorthand)
.TP
.BI \-eval " value"
Evaluate expression and exit (can be specified multiple times)
.TP
.BI \-from " string"
Input format for \-convert: sysml, kerml, ttl, turtle or rdf (default: from
the input's extension)
.TP
.B \-h
Show this help (shorthand)
.TP
.B \-help
Show this help and exit
.TP
.BI \-instantiate " value"
Create an object of this definition before the checks, so a verdict is about
it (repeatable)
.TP
.B \-json
Report checks as one JSON document rather than as lines
.TP
.B \-man
Write this command's manual page, in roff, to stdout and exit
.TP
.BI \-memprofile " string"
Write a heap profile of the run to this file, for go tool pprof
.TP
.B \-memstats
Report on stderr what the run cost: wall time, memory allocated, memory taken
from the OS
.TP
.BI \-o " string"
Write conversion output to this file (shorthand)
.TP
.BI \-output " string"
Write conversion output to this file (default: stdout)
.TP
.BI \-pdf\-engine " string"
Converter \-doc\-form pdf drives: weasyprint (default), pandoc or prince
.TP
.B \-pdf\-number\-sections
Number the section headings hierarchically (\-doc\-form pdf)
.TP
.B \-pdf\-title\-page
Put the document title on a page of its own (\-doc\-form pdf)
.TP
.B \-pdf\-toc
Write a table of contents ahead of the content (\-doc\-form pdf)
.TP
.BI \-query " string"
Evaluate OSLC Query text against the model instead of running the REPL
.TP
.B \-quiet
Report errors only, suppressing warnings
.TP
.BI \-render " string"
Render this view of the model instead of running it, in the form its render
member states
.TP
.BI \-render\-all " string"
Render every declared view into this directory
.TP
.BI \-render\-document " string"
Compile this document definition, run its queries and write the rendered
Markdown
.TP
.BI \-render\-documents " string"
Render every document definition as linked Markdown into this directory
.TP
.BI \-render\-form " string"
Form \-render or \-render\-all writes: text, mermaid or markdown (default:
destination\-dependent for \-render, each kind's machine form for
\-render\-all)
.TP
.BI \-requirement " value"
Evaluate this requirement and exit (repeatable)
.TP
.BI \-run\-query " value"
Execute this document query and report its rows, as \-run\-query
"HeavySubsystems root=telescope" (repeatable)
.TP
.B \-satisfy
Evaluate every satisfaction assertion, or with \-satisfy=<name> those the
named element states (repeatable). The default is [].
.TP
.BI \-state " value"
Run this state machine, as \-state "Mission rover1" to run it on an object
(repeatable)
.TP
.B \-strict
Judge the model as conforming SysML v2: notation no pinned production admits
is an error, not a warning
.TP
.BI \-to " value"
Replaced by \-convert, which names the output format
.TP
.B \-trace
Report each execution step: expression evaluation, calc invocation, action
tokens, state transitions
.TP
.B \-v
Show version (shorthand)
.TP
.B \-validate
Analyse the model and report its diagnostics, exiting nonzero on an error
.TP
.B \-version
Show version information
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 2
.nf
sysml                       # Start interactive REPL
sysml \-e "5 + 3"            # Evaluate and exit
sysml \-e "expr" file.sysml  # Load file, evaluate, and exit
sysml file.sysml            # Load file and start REPL
sysml \-debug file.sysml     # Load file, reporting every diagnostic
sysml \-trace file.sysml     # Load file, reporting each execution step
.fi
.RE
.SH CHECKING A MODEL
.RS 2
.nf
# Evaluate one constraint and exit
sysml \-constraint MassBudget model.sysml
# Evaluate one requirement and exit
sysml \-requirement PowerMargin model.sysml
# Evaluate every satisfaction assertion
sysml \-satisfy model.sysml
# \&...only the ones Ctx states
sysml \-satisfy=Ctx model.sysml
# Check C against an object of p
sysml \-instantiate p \-constraint C model.sysml
# Report diagnostics only
sysml \-validate model.sysml
# \&...asking whether it is conforming SysML v2
sysml \-validate \-strict model.sysml
# Invoke a calculation
sysml \-calc "Fall(3, 4)" model.sysml
# Execute a document query
sysml \-run\-query "Heavy root=scope" model.sysml
# Run an action to completion
sysml \-action Drive model.sysml
# Run a state machine for 10 time units
sysml \-state Mission \-advance 10 model.sysml
# Report the verdicts as JSON
sysml \-satisfy \-json model.sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each check flag may be repeated.
.SH CONVERSION
.RS 2
.nf
# SysML notation to RDF Turtle, on stdout
sysml model.sysml \-convert ttl
# RDF Turtle to SysML notation
sysml model.ttl \-convert sysml
# Write the conversion to a file
sysml model.sysml \-convert ttl \-o m.ttl
# Name the input format explicitly
sysml in.txt \-convert ttl \-from sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
The input format is taken from the file extension (.sysml, .kerml, .ttl)
unless \-from names it. Converting to the format it is already in rewrites the
input: notation is reformatted, Turtle is normalized.
.PP
RDF conversion is experimental: the mapping covers model structure and the
behavior its bodies state, refuses what it cannot write back, and its
vocabulary may change without a compatibility path; see
docs/reference/rdf\-mapping.md \(sc Status
.PP
Every run that converts RDF says so on stderr. Saving to .sysml or .kerml is
stable.
.SH RENDERING A VIEW
.RS 2
.nf
# ASCII text at a terminal
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-render\-form markdown
sysml model.sysml \-render Views::vehicleView \-o view.mmd
sysml model.sysml \-render\-all rendered
.fi
.RE
.PP
The rendering is the one the view's render member states, and a containment
tree where it states none. It is tool\-defined output: SysML v2 specifies the
notation, not how a tool draws it. Notices \(em an empty view, an element the
rendering cannot represent \(em go on stderr.
.SH RENDERING A DOCUMENT
.RS 2
.nf
# Markdown on stdout
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-o report.md
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form pdf \e
    \-o report.pdf
# every document, linked
sysml model.sysml \-render\-documents rendered
sysml model.sysml \-render\-document Reports::MassReport \-doc\-form pdf \e
    \-pdf\-engine pandoc \-pdf\-title\-page \-pdf\-toc \-pdf\-number\-sections \e
    \-o report.pdf
.fi
.RE
.PP
A document is a part def specializing DocumentQueries::Document. Its queries
are bound in the model and run against it, and the result is written as
CommonMark\-compatible Markdown.
.PP
\-doc\-form pdf converts that Markdown with an external converter named by
\-pdf\-engine \(em weasyprint (default), pandoc or prince \(em run as a
subprocess, never linked in; diagrams are pre\-rendered to SVG with
mermaid\-cli (mmdc). None of these tools is needed until PDF output is asked
for; scripts/download\-doc\-pdf\-toolchain.sh provisions pinned copies.
.SH FLAG ORDER
Flags may be written before or after the model they apply to. A file named
like a flag is read as a file after \-\-, which ends the flags: sysml \-trace
\-\- \-m.sysml
.SH READING FROM STANDARD INPUT
.RS 2
.nf
cat model.sysml | sysml \-validate \-  # A lone \- names standard input
cat model.sysml | sysml \- \-convert ttl \-from sysml
.fi
.RE
.PP
What was read from standard input is called <stdin> in diagnostics, and a file
really named "\-" is read by naming it ./\- instead.
.SH PROFILING A RUN
.RS 2
.nf
# Report what the run cost, on stderr
sysml \-validate \-memstats model.sysml
# Write a heap profile for go tool pprof
sysml \-validate \-memprofile heap.out model.sysml
# Write a CPU profile for go tool pprof
sysml \-validate \-cpuprofile cpu.out model.sysml
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
Every run that is not a prompt exits:
.TP
.B 0
It did what was asked.
.TP
.B 1
The model answered false for a check.
.TP
.B 2
What was asked could not be carried out at all \(em an unreadable file, a
model that did not analyse cleanly, an unresolved name, a failed conversion.
.SH OUTPUT STREAMS
What was asked for is reported on stdout and what went wrong on stderr,
prefixed "sysml: " unless it locates a finding in the source.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_LIBRARY_PATH
Directory to load the SysML/KerML standard library from, instead of the copy
embedded in the binary.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_STEPS
Expression evaluations one run may spend before it is reported as a runaway.
Default 10000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_ACTION_STEPS
Token\-flow steps one action run may perform. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_EVENTS
Events one state machine run may dispatch. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_DO_STEPS
Do actions one state machine run may perform. Default 5000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_ELEMENTS
Collection elements one evaluation may hold, which bounds the memory a run
holds rather than the work it does. Default 1000000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_MAX_CALC_DEPTH
Nested calc invocations one run may hold on the stack, which is what a
recursion spends. Default 10000, ceiling 25000.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT
Executable the %check, %explain, %solve, %configure and %optimize commands
drive as their SMT solver, speaking SMT\-LIB2 on standard input
(experimental). Unset looks for z3, then cvc5, on PATH.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT_TIMEOUT
How long one solver query may take, as a Go duration. Default 10s, after which
the verdict is unknown.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT_CORE_BUDGET
How long %explain may spend reducing an unsat core to a minimal one, as a Go
duration. Default 30s.
.TP
.B OPENSYSML_SMT_MAX_CONFIGURATIONS
How many variant selections %configure ... all may report before saying the
enumeration was cut short. Default 32.
.PP
Each variable above also answers to its legacy SYSML_\-prefixed name
(SYSML_MAX_STEPS for OPENSYSML_MAX_STEPS, and so on), which remains accepted.
When both are set and the OPENSYSML_ value is non\-empty, the OPENSYSML_ value
wins; setting only the legacy name prints a one\-time deprecation warning on
standard error.
.PP
A budget bounds one run \(em one evaluation, one instantiation, one calc
invocation, one action, one state machine \(em not a whole session, so a long
session of small operations never exhausts one. A run started inside another
shares the outer run's budget.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/sysml/history
Prompt history, when XDG_STATE_HOME is set and writable.
.TP
.B ~/.sysml_history
Prompt history otherwise. When neither can be written the history is kept for
the session only.
